# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Accession 47B Starts on the Third Kick

**Story & Context:** Tariq chooses accession number 47B from a demonstration list and turns the number into an arrangement rule: the kantele enters after four bars, the ophicleide after seven, and the first full celesta response arrives on the third kick of the next restart cycle. Aino writes a narrow Dorian ostinato that leaves one open string ringing across the triplet grid.

Marta answers with two prepared-celesta notes whose felt-damped decay lasts just long enough to fit between kick bursts. Jonas sustains a low brass tone slightly flat to the sub, sliding into alignment only when the full rhythm returns. In the middle section, Tariq removes every electronic kick and lets latch clicks, claps, and kantele taps preserve the club pulse.

The final restart is cued by an insulated-door seal thump rather than a drum fill.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Seed accession card marked 47B on a matte aluminum shelf, pale kantele strings crossing the foreground, celesta key with a felt damper, ophicleide bell reflected in a cold-room door. Spoken fragments limited to “four, seven, third kick,” “leave the string open,” “forty-seven B,” and “seal the door.”

## 2. Frost Forms Only on the Service Side

**Story & Context:** During a late session, Aino notices frost collecting on only one side of a copper service pipe because warm corridor air is leaking toward the colder room. She turns the asymmetry into a two-part kantele figure: one dry repeated note and one longer harmonic that blooms only every second cycle.

Tariq builds an unusually sparse Jersey club pattern around the difference, using triplet kicks on one half of the phrase and only handclaps on the other. Marta prepares three celesta bars with thin paper so their attacks produce faint crackling overtones. Jonas uses breath-heavy ophicleide notes with no sub underneath, making the low brass feel larger than it is.

At the peak, the electronic rhythm narrows instead of expanding: kick, clap, one seal thump, and the two-part kantele figure remain while everything else disappears.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Frost coating one side of a copper service pipe beside an insulated archive door, kantele tuning pins in close focus, three celesta bars prepared with thin paper, ophicleide mouthpiece on a wool cloth. Spoken fragments limited to “only this side,” “keep the second one long,” “no sub here,” and “let it frost.”

## 3. Shelf Stop Before the Restart

**Story & Context:** One rolling archive shelf produces a hard rubber stop sound whenever it reaches the end of its track. Tariq records the impact and places it just before every second club restart. The cue is so effective that the band begins performing around it live without needing a click.

Aino answers each stop with a descending three-note kantele figure. Marta plays short celesta dyads that end before the next kick burst, while Jonas alternates between blunt ophicleide attacks and narrow upward slides. The pattern becomes increasingly compact until the shelf-stop sample disappears entirely.

For two cycles, the group preserves the missing space where the sound used to be. On the final pass, the real shelf is rolled shut off-mic and the distant mechanical stop ends the track.

